// CIIP Academy question bank, units 6 to 10. All questions are original informatics scenarios.
window.FRA = window.FRA || {};
FRA.questions = FRA.questions || [];
FRA.questions.push(
// u6l1
{id:"u6l1-1",t:"u6l1",q:"The chair says reports are too slow, and you have defined the problem and a goal for the median turnaround. What is the NEXT step in a DMAIC project?",a:["Test a new worklist routing rule on one reading room for two weeks","Measure the current median hours from exam complete to final report, stage by stage","Hold a fishbone session to rank the root causes of the delay","Set up a monthly run chart with a named owner and a trigger"],c:1,e:"DMAIC runs define, measure, analyze, improve, control. After the problem and goal are defined, you collect a baseline. Testing a fix, ranking causes, and building a monitor are later steps, and skipping to them means changing a process you have not yet measured."},
{id:"u6l1-2",t:"u6l1",q:"A team tried technologist release on completion at one scanner for two weeks and the median fell. In PDSA, what should they do FIRST with the result?",a:["Write a policy that requires release on completion across the department","Roll the new release rule out to every scanner and every site at once","Compare the measured result with the prediction made in the plan","Replace the pilot with a full redesign"],c:2,e:"The study step comes right after the do step: compare what happened with what you predicted. Only after that do you act, by adopting, adjusting, or dropping the change. Rolling out first skips the learning that makes small cycles safe."},
{id:"u6l1-3",t:"u6l1",q:"A value stream map shows finished CT studies sit three hours before a technologist releases them to the worklist. Which Lean waste does this show?",a:["Unused talent, because technologists are not asked for process ideas","Defects, because the studies contain errors that must be corrected before release","Over-processing, because each study is checked by two different people","Waiting between steps, idle time a value stream map exposes"],c:3,e:"Lean counts idle time between steps as waiting waste. The map draws each step and the time work sits unattended between steps, so the long idle bar points to where to look. Nothing in the scenario suggests errors, double checks, or ignored ideas."},
{id:"u6l1-4",t:"u6l1",q:"One radiologist's reports often miss the turnaround target. The manager proposes a written warning to that person. Which response fits the philosophy of quality improvement?",a:["Examine the steps and handoffs every report passes through, because the system produces the delay","Warn the radiologist in writing, then re-measure that person's turnaround at the end of next month","Move the radiologist's studies to a colleague and stop measuring individual times","Ask the vendor to add an alert that flags slow reports to the chair"],c:0,e:"Quality improvement treats delays as products of the process, so you map the handoffs, queues, and tools first. Blaming one person fixes nothing if the same pathway delays everyone's reports, and measuring is how you tell whether the pathway improved."},
{id:"u6l1-5",t:"u6l1",q:"After a release-on-completion change cut the median report time, which activity belongs to the Control step of DMAIC?",a:["Collecting four weeks of baseline timestamps for each stage of the report journey, one scanner at a time","Charting the monthly median with a named owner and a trigger to reconvene if it rises","Piloting a second routing rule on one scanner for two weeks and comparing the median before and after","Running a fishbone session to find what else slows the reports"],c:1,e:"Control keeps the gain: a chart, an owner, and a rule for when to act. Baseline collection is Measure, the pilot is Improve, and the fishbone session is Analyze. Without control, a process drifts back to its old performance."},

// u6l2
{id:"u6l2-1",t:"u6l2",q:"You phone the PACS manager about an empty CT worklist. Which sentence belongs in the Recommendation part of SBAR?",a:["The order interface was upgraded last night and nothing else in the department changed","The CT worklist has been empty since 7:10 this morning, and no studies have reached the technologists","Restart the order interface now, or approve manual worklist entries for the next hour","The broker is probably holding messages behind one bad order"],c:2,e:"SBAR is situation, background, assessment, recommendation. The recommendation is what you want done and by when. The empty worklist is the situation, last night's upgrade is the background, and your guess about the queued bad message is the assessment."},
{id:"u6l2-2",t:"u6l2",q:"This week's exception queue holds 140 studies: name format mismatch 84, accession errors 28, procedure code errors 17, other 11. Which approach removes the most of the problem for the effort?",a:["Fix the other category first, since it has no clear owner on the team","Fix the accession errors first, since the second bar is the easiest to explain to users","Fix the procedure code errors first, since the matching rule for them is simple to change","Fix the name format mismatch first, the largest bar of a Pareto chart"],c:3,e:"A Pareto chart ranks causes by size so the first bars get the work. Name mismatch is 60 percent of the queue, and the first two causes together are 80 percent. Choosing by ease or ownership ignores where the volume really is."},
{id:"u6l2-3",t:"u6l2",q:"Your team meets to list every possible reason that the viewer is slow, and you want the ideas grouped so none is forgotten. Which tool fits?",a:["A fishbone chart grouping causes by people, process, equipment, software, and environment","A run chart plotting the daily viewer load time for the past month","A Pareto chart ranking last week's viewer complaints by their reason","An SBAR message sent to the department chair with a recommendation"],c:0,e:"The fishbone (cause-and-effect) chart is the brainstorming tool: the problem sits at the head and possible causes are grouped on the bones. A Pareto and a run chart need data you do not yet have, and SBAR is for telling someone, not for finding causes."},
{id:"u6l2-4",t:"u6l2",q:"A radiologist says the viewer is slow. Asking why repeatedly leads from a cold cache to a prefetch rule that failed, to a changed schedule feed, to an untested change. What does five whys give you?",a:["A ranked list of causes by how many studies each one affected over the month","A root cause that can be fixed, not a symptom such as slowness","A chart of load time that separates normal variation from a real change in it","A message that tells the PACS manager what you need and by when"],c:1,e:"Five whys keeps asking why until you reach a cause you can act on, here a change made without checking the affected systems. Ranking is the Pareto chart, limits belong to a control chart, and telling someone is SBAR."},
{id:"u6l2-5",t:"u6l2",q:"Median report turnaround moves between 6.2 and 6.9 hours from month to month. The manager asks whether this month's 6.9 is a real change. Which tool answers that?",a:["A fishbone chart of possible reasons for slower reports across the department","A Pareto chart ranking the stages of the report journey by hours spent in each","A control chart whose limits separate normal variation from a real change","A pivot table of studies by modality and month"],c:2,e:"A control chart adds upper and lower limits to a run chart, so a point inside the limits is ordinary variation and a point outside is a signal. The other tools rank, brainstorm, or summarize, and none of them judges variation."},

// u6l3
{id:"u6l3-1",t:"u6l3",q:"An audit finds three accounts that belong to staff who left the department last year and are still active. Which control was missing or skipped?",a:["The reconciliation step that closes a downtime event","The restore test schedule in the backup and recovery policy, which sets how often copies are proven to work","The severity levels and escalation chain in the incident management policy for reported problems","The periodic access review and removal of leavers in user management"],c:3,e:"Leaver accounts survive when termination is not triggered and the periodic review does not catch them. Backup tests, incident severity, and downtime reconciliation are real policy parts, but none of them removes accounts."},
{id:"u6l3-2",t:"u6l3",q:"The downtime plan is a 30 page document, and the night technologists cannot find the steps during an outage. What is the BEST fix?",a:["Write a short numbered procedure with an owner for each step and post it at each console and in the on-call binder","Email the full document to all night staff every quarter","Add an appendix that lists every failure the department has had","Move the document to a faster file share"],c:0,e:"A procedure staff will follow is short, in the order of the work, names who does each step, and sits where the work happens. Making a long document easier to send, longer, or faster to open does not make it usable at 2 in the morning."},
{id:"u6l3-3",t:"u6l3",q:"Which set of contents belongs in a backup and recovery policy?",a:["Which roles may view patient studies and how access is requested and removed from staff","Scope, frequency, copy location, retention, and restore testing","The severity levels, response targets, and escalation for a reported problem to the vendor","Which workstation or viewer staff use while the primary system is unavailable"],c:1,e:"A backup policy states scope, frequency, location, retention, and restore testing. The other options describe the access policy, the incident management policy, and the downtime policy, which are separate documents with their own owners."},
{id:"u6l3-4",t:"u6l3",q:"A policy approved three years ago has a yearly review date, and an incident last month exposed a gap in it. When should the policy be reviewed?",a:["Only when the vendor releases a new software version for the system","At the next scheduled review, since the date was set when the policy was approved","Now, after the incident, as well as at the scheduled review date","After the governance committee changes its membership"],c:2,e:"A review date is a floor, not a ceiling: a real incident that exposed a gap triggers an early review. Waiting for the calendar, a vendor release, or a committee change leaves a known weakness in place."},
{id:"u6l3-5",t:"u6l3",q:"A technologist moves to the PACS administration team. What is the BEST access action?",a:["Leave the account unchanged until the next yearly access review","Keep the technologist role and add the administrator role to the same account","Copy the rights of the administrator she is replacing into a custom account for her","Assign the administrator role and remove the technologist role at the change of job"],c:3,e:"Access follows the job, and a job change is a trigger for the access lifecycle: assign the new role and remove the old one at once. Stacking roles and custom copies create excess access, and waiting for the yearly review leaves the wrong rights in place."},

// u6l4
{id:"u6l4-1",t:"u6l4",q:"The audit log shows a radiologist opened the study of a neighbor who is not her patient. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Preserve the audit log entries and report to the privacy officer, who decides whether it is a reportable breach","Tell the neighbor directly that her record was opened, before anyone has reviewed it","Disable the radiologist's account permanently before any review takes place","Retrain the radiologist and close the matter without a report"],c:0,e:"Informatics preserves the evidence and hands the decision to the privacy officer, because the notification clock runs from discovery and the legal call is not yours. Notifying the patient, firing, or quietly closing the matter all skip that step."},
{id:"u6l4-2",t:"u6l4",q:"Which pairing of a law and its main subject is correct?",a:["The HIPAA Security Rule with the retention of mammograms and mammography reports","HITECH with breach notification and stronger penalties","MQSA with the encryption of electronic patient data in storage and transit","The HIPAA Privacy Rule with the accreditation of mammography facilities"],c:1,e:"HITECH added breach notification and stronger enforcement, and extended HIPAA to business associates. MQSA is the federal mammography law covering accreditation and records, while the HIPAA rules protect health information and its safeguards."},
{id:"u6l4-3",t:"u6l4",q:"A breach is discovered on March 1. Under the HIPAA Breach Notification Rule, what is the latest point for notifying affected individuals?",a:["30 days after discovery","90 days after discovery","60 days after discovery","120 days after discovery"],c:2,e:"Individuals must be told without unreasonable delay and no later than 60 days after discovery. Waiting the full period is not the goal; the 60 days is a ceiling, and the clock runs from discovery, not from the end of the investigation."},
{id:"u6l4-4",t:"u6l4",q:"Which duty falls to the informatics team under MQSA?",a:["Calculating each patient's radiation dose by hand from the technique factors on the console","Interpreting mammograms for the facility when its radiologist is unavailable that day","Certifying that each interpreting physician holds the required qualifications and training","Keeping mammograms, reports, and display QC records for the retention period"],c:3,e:"Informatics keeps the archive's retention rules correct so no automatic delete removes a mammography study early, and keeps the display quality control records an inspector asks for. Reading, certifying physicians, and dose calculation belong to others."},
{id:"u6l4-5",t:"u6l4",q:"A dose tracking system will store values from every CT. Which task belongs to the informatics team?",a:["Make sure every dose-producing modality sends its report, map values to exam types, and flag outliers for physicist review","Decide whether a patient's dose was clinically appropriate and change the protocol","Calculate each patient's dose by hand from the technique factors on the console","Set the reference levels for each exam type without physicist input"],c:0,e:"Informatics owns the data flow: each modality sends its radiation dose structured report, values map to the right exam type, comparison with reference levels is automatic, and outliers go to a physicist. Judging appropriateness and changing protocols is clinical."},

// u6l5
{id:"u6l5-1",t:"u6l5",q:"The vendor will patch the PACS on Tuesday morning. Which item must exist BEFORE the change begins?",a:["A post-change review summary signed by the section chief who requested the change","A back-out plan with the point at which it will be used","A list of defects found after go-live, sorted by the severity that users report","A message to users reporting that the change is complete and verified"],c:1,e:"The back-out plan is written before the change, so you know how long a failure can last and when to return to the old state. A review and a completion notice come after, and defects found later cannot be listed in advance."},
{id:"u6l5-2",t:"u6l5",q:"Which item belongs in the operating budget rather than the capital budget?",a:["Purchase of 24 diagnostic displays for the new reading room and its workstations","Purchase of a new archive hardware set expected to last several years in the data center","Annual support and maintenance on the existing PACS","Construction of a new reading room with wiring and furniture"],c:2,e:"Operating spending is the recurring cost to run what you already own: support, licenses, storage growth, and staff. Capital buys long-lived assets such as hardware and displays, and finance sets exactly where the line falls."},
{id:"u6l5-3",t:"u6l5",q:"A server fails at night and an emergency change is needed to restore service. What is the correct handling?",a:["Apply it and announce the change to users only if they ask","Apply it now and skip documentation, since the outage is the priority for everyone","Wait for the weekly change board so that approval is not bypassed in any case","A named approver authorizes it quickly, and it is documented and reviewed afterward"],c:3,e:"Emergency changes may be approved fast by a named person, but they still get written up and reviewed. Skipping the record, waiting for the board, or staying silent each leave the change invisible to the next person who troubleshoots."},
{id:"u6l5-4",t:"u6l5",q:"A new CT scanner will double the daily study volume. Which operating budget line is MOST affected?",a:["Storage growth and the capacity purchase schedule","Training for reading room staff on the new scanner","License renewals for each reading seat","Support and maintenance on the old PACS, which is renewed by contract each year"],c:0,e:"Doubling daily volume doubles the rate at which capacity is consumed, so months remaining shrinks and storage must be bought sooner. The other lines may move slightly, but the storage line is the one directly driven by study volume."},
{id:"u6l5-5",t:"u6l5",q:"The change board receives a request to alter the order interface. What does the impact assessment determine?",a:["Which section is most eager to have the change made, and how soon it wants it","Which systems, interfaces, and users are affected and what could go wrong","How the post-change review will be scored by the board after the change","Which vendor will be asked to carry out the change on the board's behalf"],c:1,e:"The impact assessment lists what the change touches and the risk to each, so the board can approve, schedule, and plan a back-out with open eyes. Enthusiasm, scoring of the review, and the choice of who does the work are not impact."},

// u6l6
{id:"u6l6-1",t:"u6l6",q:"The interface queue depth KPI grows steadily through the morning. What does that indicate?",a:["A rise in studies that are unread past the target time on the reading worklist","A storage capacity shortage that is slowing the delivery of messages to the archive","A stalled interface receiving messages faster than it delivers them","A drop in uptime caused by a planned maintenance window"],c:2,e:"Queue depth counts messages waiting for delivery. A queue that keeps growing means the receiving side has slowed or stopped, which is why a stalled interface is the first suspect. Capacity, unread exams, and maintenance have their own KPIs."},
{id:"u6l6-2",t:"u6l6",q:"A 100 TB archive has 71 TB used and grows 2 TB each month. How many months remain before it is full?",a:["58 months","29 months","35.5 months","14.5 months"],c:3,e:"Months remaining is free space divided by monthly growth: 100 minus 71 is 29 TB free, and 29 divided by 2 is 14.5. The value 29 forgets to divide, 35.5 divides used space by growth, and 58 multiplies instead of dividing."},
{id:"u6l6-3",t:"u6l6",q:"The monthly report shows an average report turnaround of 5.8 hours, yet radiologists complain of long waits. What should the report add?",a:["The median and a high percentile, such as the slowest tenth","A second average, calculated separately for each modality in the department","The number of reports finalized each day this month, shown as a total","The count of unread exams at the end of the month, split by priority"],c:0,e:"An average can sit comfortably within target while a long tail of slow reports annoys people. The median and a high percentile expose the tail, and they are the measures that match the complaint."},
{id:"u6l6-4",t:"u6l6",q:"You are building the monthly report for the hospital executives. Which design is BEST?",a:["Interface queue depth by hour with the message counts for each interface","Uptime, risk, and cost on one page, with a line explaining each number outside its target","Every KPI with the raw log extracts attached for reference","Unread exams by section in a daily table"],c:1,e:"Executives want a page they can read in a minute: availability, risk, and cost, with an explanation and an action for anything off target. Queue-by-hour detail suits IT and daily tables suit the department."},
{id:"u6l6-5",t:"u6l6",q:"Which description fits a key performance indicator in imaging operations?",a:["A chart that summarizes exams by modality and month for the manager","Any number exported from a system log each month for the department","A measure with a definition, a target, and an owner","A target stated in the vendor's service agreement and reported each quarter"],c:2,e:"A KPI is useful only when its definition is fixed, its target is known, and someone owns it. A raw log number, a chart, or a vendor target alone is not an indicator of how your operation is doing."},

// u6l7
{id:"u6l7-1",t:"u6l7",q:"A manager asks which modality has grown fastest over 24 months, and you have the full exam list. Which approach answers it?",a:["A dashboard of live queue counts and unread exams","A scatter plot of study size against transfer time for every exam in the list","A bar chart of this month's exam counts for each modality in the department","A pivot of modality by month, drawn as a line chart"],c:3,e:"Growth is change over time, so pivot the list by modality and month and plot lines. A bar chart of this month shows size, not growth; a scatter plot shows the relation of two measures; a dashboard shows the present."},
{id:"u6l7-2",t:"u6l7",q:"A bar chart compares two sites at 98 percent and 96 percent on-time reports, drawn on an axis from 95 to 100. What is wrong with it?",a:["The truncated axis exaggerates a two point difference; start at zero or label the values","The sites should be joined by a line to show the trend between them over time","The chart needs a third color to separate the sites more clearly for readers","The two percentages should be averaged into one bar for the group"],c:0,e:"An axis that does not start at zero makes small differences look large. Start at zero or show the numbers, and say how many exams each site read. Joining categories with a line implies an order that does not exist."},
{id:"u6l7-3",t:"u6l7",q:"Which question is a scatter plot suited to answer?",a:["Which site sent the most exceptions to the queue last month?","Do larger studies take proportionally longer to transfer?","How did monthly volume change over the last year in each modality?","Is the interface queue normal right now?"],c:1,e:"A scatter plot places each item by two measures, so it shows whether they move together, such as images against seconds. Counting by site calls for bars, change over time calls for lines, and live status calls for a dashboard."},
{id:"u6l7-4",t:"u6l7",q:"You are designing a dashboard for the on-call analyst. Which design is BEST?",a:["Monthly averages for the past three years, shown by modality and by each site","Every measure available, so that nothing is hidden from the analyst on call","Few measures needing action, flagged against limits, with last refresh time","One large pivot table of the full exam list, refreshed once a week by hand from an export"],c:2,e:"A dashboard supports monitoring, so it shows what someone must act on, flags problems, and states how fresh the data is. A wall of measures, long averages, or a raw pivot table do not tell the analyst what to do now."},
{id:"u6l7-5",t:"u6l7",q:"A scatter plot shows long transfer times from one site at every study size. What is the BEST interpretation?",a:["Transfer time is unrelated to anything and should be dropped as a KPI","Larger studies cause long transfers at every site, and the site is not involved","The scatter plot is misleading and should be replaced by a bar chart of the sites","Probably the site, such as its network link; investigate there"],c:3,e:"If the delay appears at every size at one site, size does not explain it; something at the site does, perhaps the link. A chart points you toward a cause but you still investigate it, and the pattern is evidence, not noise."},

// u6l8
{id:"u6l8-1",t:"u6l8",q:"A vendor's SLA promises a one hour response for critical issues, and a critical outage lasts nine hours. Which SLA term was missing?",a:["A resolution time target with a remedy when it is missed","An uptime guarantee that the vendor measures for itself and reports each quarter","A response time for noncritical issues, listed by severity level","A cap on the renewal price increase"],c:0,e:"Response is when work begins; resolution is when the problem is fixed or worked around. A promise of response alone is met even in a nine hour outage, so the agreement needs a resolution target and a remedy."},
{id:"u6l8-2",t:"u6l8",q:"Four months before expiry, the PACS vendor quotes a 9 percent increase. What is the BEST action?",a:["Accept, since annual increases are standard and negotiation rarely works","Compare your incident and uptime log with the SLA, ask for credits owed, and get competing quotes","Let the contract renew automatically and raise the price next year","Ask the vendor to send its own uptime report as the basis"],c:1,e:"Renewal is leverage, and it rests on your own records: response times, resolution times, and uptime from your monitoring. A vendor-supplied report can leave out the incidents you care about, and auto-renewal can lock the price in."},
{id:"u6l8-3",t:"u6l8",q:"The vendor announces end of service life for your PACS in 18 months. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Order a replacement from the same vendor immediately, without an evaluation","Wait until the final date, since the system keeps running without support","Learn the end date, extended support cost, and migration path, then plan","Cancel the support agreement now to save money, since the product is ending"],c:2,e:"An end of life notice starts a replacement project: confirm the date, learn what extended support costs, and find the path to the next system, with budget planned early. Waiting leaves you unsupported, and buying blindly skips the evaluation."},
{id:"u6l8-4",t:"u6l8",q:"Which document states exactly what the vendor will deliver, by when, who does what, and how completion is accepted?",a:["The renewal notice, which lists next year's price","The support and maintenance agreement, which covers updates, hot fixes, and support hours after go-live","The service level agreement, which sets response times and uptime promises","The statement of work, which belongs to the project and lists deliverables and acceptance"],c:3,e:"The SOW defines the project work and its acceptance. The SMA covers support after go-live and the SLA covers service promises and remedies, while a renewal notice is a pricing document."},
{id:"u6l8-5",t:"u6l8",q:"A vendor's SLA promises 99.5 percent uptime. How many hours of downtime a year does it allow?",a:["43.8 hours","8.8 hours","87.6 hours","4.4 hours"],c:0,e:"A year has 8,760 hours, and 0.5 percent of that is 43.8 hours. The value 8.8 is the allowance at 99.9 percent, 87.6 is the allowance at 99 percent, and 4.4 is the allowance at 99.95 percent."},

// u7l1
{id:"u7l1-1",t:"u7l1",q:"A system costs 300,000 and is expected to save 60,000 a year for 6 years. What is the return on investment?",a:["16.7 percent","20 percent","120 percent","83.3 percent"],c:1,e:"ROI is (gain minus cost) divided by cost, times 100. The gain is 60,000 x 6 = 360,000, so (360,000 minus 300,000) / 300,000 = 20 percent. The value 16.7 divides by the gain, 120 is gain over cost, and 83.3 is cost over gain."},
{id:"u7l1-2",t:"u7l1",q:"A project costs 500,000 and saves 125,000 a year. What is the payback period?",a:["0.25 years","25 years","4 years","8 years"],c:2,e:"Payback is initial cost divided by annual savings: 500,000 / 125,000 = 4 years. The value 0.25 inverts the division, 25 reads the savings as a percent of cost, and 8 has no basis in the figures."},
{id:"u7l1-3",t:"u7l1",q:"The board favors the lowest quote, but a higher bid has lower support and storage costs. What should you present to justify the higher bid?",a:["The vendor's list of installed customers at sites of a similar size","The return on investment calculated from the purchase price alone","The payback period of the lower quote","Total cost of ownership over the system's life, including support, storage, staff, training, and migration"],c:3,e:"TCO counts everything the system costs over its life, so a bid that costs more to buy can cost less to own. ROI from the price alone repeats the same blind spot, and a customer list does not address cost."},
{id:"u7l1-4",t:"u7l1",q:"When should the preliminary budget for a procurement be set?",a:["Before requests go to vendors, so proposals can be judged against a ceiling","After vendors submit prices, based on the lowest bid received from any of them","After contract negotiation, once the final price is known to both parties","After go-live, when actual costs are known"],c:0,e:"A preliminary budget is part of the project definition, agreed with the goals and scope before any request is issued. Setting it from bids or after the contract removes its purpose, which is to tell reasonable proposals from unrealistic ones."},
{id:"u7l1-5",t:"u7l1",q:"The vendor ends support for your PACS in 12 months, and the replacement has no dollar return. What is the BEST justification?",a:["A return of 0 percent, so the project should be deferred until a measurable dollar return can be shown to the board","The risk of running an unsupported system that cannot be fixed if it fails, with the replacement's TCO","A payback period shorter than the vendor's notice period","A survey showing radiologists like the viewer"],c:1,e:"Some projects are justified by risk, not return. An unsupported archive cannot be repaired when it fails, so the case states that risk and the cost of ownership of the replacement. A zero return is not a reason to defer a required replacement."},

// u7l2
{id:"u7l2-1",t:"u7l2",q:"A department asks you to buy a new viewer by name. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Ask finance for a budget based on the price of that viewer","Send an RFP to the three vendors that make the viewer, with the name of the product","Interview and observe users to find the problem the viewer is meant to solve","Schedule demonstrations from each viewer vendor"],c:2,e:"A request for a product may hide a different need, such as slow access to prior studies. Needs assessment comes before the request: interview, observe, document, analyze, prioritize, and validate."},
{id:"u7l2-2",t:"u7l2",q:"Which requirement can a vendor answer yes or no and you can test?",a:["The viewer should integrate well with the systems we run","The system should be fast and easy for radiologists to use","The vendor must provide excellent support after go-live","A 500-image CT study opens at the workstation within the stated time during the scripted demonstration"],c:3,e:"A testable requirement names a condition, a measure, and a way to check it. Words like fast, excellent, and well cannot be answered or tested, so every vendor will claim to meet them."},
{id:"u7l2-3",t:"u7l2",q:"You need prices for 20 identical diagnostic displays with a defined specification. Which request fits?",a:["An RFQ, since the item is defined and only price and delivery are needed","An RFI to learn which vendors sell displays and what models they offer","An RFP with weighted criteria for the whole imaging solution and its support","A survey of radiologists about display preferences"],c:0,e:"A request for quotation asks for a price on a clearly defined item. An RFI is for learning the market, and an RFP is for selecting a complete solution with detailed requirements and weighted criteria."},
{id:"u7l2-4",t:"u7l2",q:"You have documented requirements from the interviews. What comes next in needs assessment?",a:["Send the list to vendors as the RFP, along with the evaluation criteria","Analyze it for conflicts and duplicates, then prioritize","Validate the list with the users who gave it to you","Choose a vendor shortlist"],c:1,e:"The order is collect, document, analyze, prioritize, and validate. Analysis removes conflicts and duplicates, and prioritization separates must-have from nice-to-have. Validation and the vendor request come after."},
{id:"u7l2-5",t:"u7l2",q:"How should the finalized RFP be distributed?",a:["To each vendor with requirements tailored to its product","To the preferred vendor first, so its questions shape the final version","The identical package to every vendor at the same time, with questions and answers shared with all","Only to vendors that ask for it after the deadline"],c:2,e:"Fair evaluation needs equal information. Every vendor gets the same package at the same time, and clarifications go to all of them. Tailoring or early access makes the proposals incomparable and invites challenge."},

// u7l3
{id:"u7l3-1",t:"u7l3",q:"Why does a procurement team use scripted demonstrations?",a:["The scenarios are chosen by each vendor to match the strengths of its product","Each vendor shows its strongest features in the order it prefers","The vendor's own engineer can configure the system before the visit","Every vendor performs the same scenarios on your sample studies"],c:3,e:"A script makes the vendors do the same things, so scores compare like with like. Vendor-chosen demos compare sales skill, not products."},
{id:"u7l3-2",t:"u7l3",q:"When should the weights of a scoring matrix be set?",a:["Before proposals are scored, so the weights cannot be steered toward a favorite","After scoring, to reflect which vendor the team preferred at the end of the process","By the vendor with the highest score, who knows its own strengths best","After the contract is signed"],c:0,e:"Weights agreed in advance keep the evaluation honest. Setting them after seeing the scores lets the team tune the result toward a favorite, and a vendor has no role in choosing them."},
{id:"u7l3-3",t:"u7l3",q:"A matrix weights clinical function 40, cost 30, and support 30. A vendor scores 5, 3, and 4 on a scale of 1 to 5. What is its weighted total?",a:["400","410","12","82"],c:1,e:"Multiply each score by its weight and add: 5 x 40 = 200, 3 x 30 = 90, 4 x 30 = 120, total 410 of a possible 500. The value 400 averages the scores and scales them, 12 adds the scores unweighted, and 82 is the total as a percent of 500."},
{id:"u7l3-4",t:"u7l3",q:"Which approach to reference checks is BEST?",a:["Read the vendor's published case studies, which describe its best installations","Call only the customers the vendor lists and confirm that they are satisfied with the product","Contact customers of your size and type, including some you choose, and ask what went wrong","Ask for its satisfaction score"],c:2,e:"Reference calls work when they reach comparable sites and ask about problems, not praise. A list chosen by the vendor, its case studies, and its own satisfaction score are all controlled by the vendor."},
{id:"u7l3-5",t:"u7l3",q:"Which contract clause protects you if you leave the vendor in five years?",a:["A table of response times by severity","A payment schedule tied to project milestones, with the last payment held back","Acceptance criteria for the go-live tests, written into the contract","Data ownership and exit terms: the data stays yours and is returned in a standard format"],c:3,e:"Exit terms protect your ability to change vendors: you own the data and get it back in a standard format with defined help. Payment schedules, acceptance criteria, and response times matter, but none of them lets you leave."},

// u8l1
{id:"u8l1-1",t:"u8l1",q:"Midway through an upgrade, a section asks for extra hanging protocol layouts. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Have the request submitted in writing and estimate its effect on cost, time, and risk","Add it to the plan quietly, since the layouts are small and the team can absorb them","Decline it, because all scope is closed once the contract is signed by both sides","Ask the vendor to include it at no charge"],c:0,e:"Scope change control starts with a written request and an estimate of its effect; the sponsor or board then approves, declines, or defers it, and the plan is updated only after approval. Quiet yes and blanket no both bypass the process."},
{id:"u8l1-2",t:"u8l1",q:"A key interface analyst might resign, and the storage vendor might deliver late. How are these risks classified?",a:["Both are internal because both would affect the project and its schedule","The resignation is internal and the vendor delay is external","The resignation is external and the vendor delay is internal to the project","Both are external because neither is under the project manager's control"],c:1,e:"Internal risks come from within the project's own resources, such as staff. External risks come from outside it, such as a supplier's delivery. External risks are reduced through contract dates and early warning."},
{id:"u8l1-3",t:"u8l1",q:"Which set of fields belongs in a risk register entry?",a:["Budget, actual cost, forecast, and variance for each month of the project","Start date, end date, resource, and predecessor for each task in the plan","Likelihood, impact, owner, and mitigation","Requester, effort, schedule effect, and decision"],c:2,e:"A risk register records how likely each risk is, how bad it would be, who owns it, and what will be done. The other sets describe a task schedule, a cost report, and a scope change request."},
{id:"u8l1-4",t:"u8l1",q:"The contract is changed to charge the vendor a penalty for late storage delivery. Which risk response is this?",a:["Reduce, because the delivery is now certain","Avoid, because the plan no longer depends on the delivery of the storage","Accept, because the delay is still expected to occur on the same date","Transfer, because part of the cost of the delay moves to the vendor"],c:3,e:"Transfer shifts the cost of a risk to another party through contract terms or insurance. The delay can still happen, so the risk is not removed or avoided, and the team has not simply accepted it."},
{id:"u8l1-5",t:"u8l1",q:"The migration is scheduled for Monday, but the storage will not be installed until Wednesday. What does this show?",a:["A dependency: the migration cannot start until storage is installed, so the plan must move","A scope change that must go to the change board and be approved before the migration begins","A milestone that has been reached early and needs no action","A risk to be accepted and left in the register"],c:0,e:"A dependency is a task that cannot start until another finishes. Because the predecessor is late, the successor and everything behind it must be rescheduled, which is why dependencies are recorded."},

// u8l2
{id:"u8l2-1",t:"u8l2",q:"Which description fits a work breakdown structure?",a:["A timeline of task bars with dependencies and milestones drawn across the project dates","The project split into smaller pieces until each can be owned and estimated","A list of risks with the likelihood and impact of each, and its owner and mitigation","The longest chain of dependent tasks, which sets the shortest possible schedule"],c:1,e:"A WBS is an outline of deliverables, broken down until a work package is small enough for one person to own and estimate. The timeline is a Gantt chart, the risks are the register, and the longest chain is the critical path."},
{id:"u8l2-2",t:"u8l2",q:"A task needs 16 hours of effort, and the assigned analyst can give it 4 hours a day. How many working days will it take?",a:["16 days","2 days","4 days","64 days"],c:2,e:"Duration is effort divided by the time available per day: 16 / 4 = 4 working days. The value 2 assumes a full 8 hour day, 16 treats hours as days, and 64 multiplies instead of dividing."},
{id:"u8l2-3",t:"u8l2",q:"Task A takes 4 days, B takes 6, C takes 5 and needs A, and D takes 2 and needs both B and C. How long is the critical path?",a:["8 days","9 days","17 days","11 days"],c:3,e:"The paths are A-C-D = 4 + 5 + 2 = 11 days and B-D = 6 + 2 = 8 days. The longest is the critical path at 11. The value 17 adds every task as if they ran in sequence, and 9 stops at A and C."},
{id:"u8l2-4",t:"u8l2",q:"Which item is a milestone?",a:["Test system ready, a major result with no duration of its own","Configure hanging protocols, estimated at 80 hours of analyst time","Migrate prior studies, which runs for three weeks in the evenings","Train super-users, which needs two analysts"],c:0,e:"A milestone marks a major result and has no duration. The other items are tasks with effort, duration, or resources."},
{id:"u8l2-5",t:"u8l2",q:"A task that is not on the critical path has 3 days of slack and slips by 2 days. What happens to the end date?",a:["The end date slips by two days, the full length of the delay in the task","The end date does not move, and one day of slack remains","The end date slips by three days, the full length of the slack available","The end date slips by one day"],c:1,e:"Slack is the time a task can slip without delaying the project. A 2 day delay uses 2 of 3 days, so the end date holds and 1 day remains. Only delays on the critical path, or beyond the slack, move the end date."},

// u8l3
{id:"u8l3-1",t:"u8l3",q:"Which tool shows tasks, dependencies, and milestones across dates?",a:["A fishbone chart of the causes of delay in each task","A Pareto chart ranking tasks by the hours spent on them","A Gantt chart","A pivot table of effort by role"],c:2,e:"A Gantt chart draws a bar for each task on a timeline, with dependencies and milestones. It answers what is happening, what is next, and what is late."},
{id:"u8l3-2",t:"u8l3",q:"Which five areas does a regular project status report cover?",a:["Uptime, capacity, exceptions, unread exams, and lost studies","Scope, vendor, contract, risk, and training for the whole project","Goals, scope, risks, dependencies, and milestones, as listed in the plan","Schedule, budget, cost, resources, and quality"],c:3,e:"A status report covers schedule, budget, cost, resources, and quality, plus top risks and decisions needed. The last option lists operational KPIs, which belong to a different report."},
{id:"u8l3-3",t:"u8l3",q:"A task estimated at 70 hours has used 65 hours and is 60 percent complete. What total effort does it project?",a:["108 hours","70 hours","65 hours","38 hours"],c:0,e:"Divide hours spent by the fraction done: 65 / 0.6 = about 108 hours. The value 70 is the original estimate, 65 is what has been spent, and 38 is the overrun, not the total."},
{id:"u8l3-4",t:"u8l3",q:"A signed charter that names the sponsor and project manager belongs to which process group?",a:["Planning, which builds the WBS, schedule, and budget for the project","Initiating: it authorizes the project and names its sponsor","Executing, which carries out the work and collects test results","Closing, which obtains acceptance and records the lessons learned"],c:1,e:"Initiating produces the charter that authorizes the project. Planning builds the WBS and schedule, executing does the work, and closing obtains acceptance."},
{id:"u8l3-5",t:"u8l3",q:"In week 8, interfaces are 5 days late and a critical defect is open, though spending is on plan. What should the status report do?",a:["Report the project as on track, since the budget is on plan","Hold the report until the interfaces are back on schedule","State the slip now, with an owner and fix date for the defect and the decision needed from the sponsor","Describe it in the closing report"],c:2,e:"Early honest reporting gives the sponsor time to help. The report should show the schedule slip, the critical defect with an owner and a date, and the decision required, rather than hide the problem behind a good budget line."},

// u8l4
{id:"u8l4-1",t:"u8l4",q:"On the Thursday before cutover, three modalities fail connectivity tests. What is the BEST action?",a:["Have the vendor sign a statement that the modalities will work","Go live as scheduled and fix the modalities in the first week","Cancel the project and ask the vendor for a refund","Check the open item against the go-live criteria, decide go or no-go at the meeting, and record the reasons"],c:3,e:"Go-live uses criteria agreed in advance. An open critical item is weighed at the go/no-go meeting, and either a fix, a documented exception, or a delay is chosen and recorded. A promise does not make the modalities connect."},
{id:"u8l4-2",t:"u8l4",q:"When should the back-out plan be retired after go-live?",a:["When a named decision confirms that the system is stable","Immediately after the cutover completes and the first study is read","After the first radiologist reads a study on the new system","At the end of the first day"],c:0,e:"The back-out plan stays ready until the team agrees on a named day or decision that the system is stable. Retiring it at cutover removes the safety net when problems are most likely."},
{id:"u8l4-3",t:"u8l4",q:"What is the purpose of a project retrospective?",a:["To assign blame for the problems that occurred","To review what went well and what did not, and write lessons learned with owners","To obtain the sponsor's acceptance signature and release the final payment to the vendor","To compare actual hours with the estimate for the final invoice and the audit"],c:1,e:"A retrospective asks what went well, what did not, and what to change, and records actions with owners. Acceptance and invoicing are closing tasks, and blame teaches nothing."},
{id:"u8l4-4",t:"u8l4",q:"What makes a lessons-learned record useful?",a:["It is kept in the project manager's personal notes so that it can be shared on request","It is written by the vendor and sent to the sponsor in a formal report when the project closes","It sits in a shared place with an owner per action, and is read at the next kickoff","It is presented at the go-live meeting and then archived in the project folder for audit"],c:2,e:"Lessons help only if the next team can find them, someone owns each action, and the file is read when planning begins. Private notes and archived slides are not used again."},
{id:"u8l4-5",t:"u8l4",q:"Which support plan fits the first days after go-live?",a:["A single analyst on call for the whole site","Normal help-desk hours with the vendor's phone number posted","Vendor email support only, reviewed weekly","Analysts and super-users in the reading rooms on every shift, a direct line, and a daily issue review"],c:3,e:"Support is heaviest at the start: people at the elbow on every shift, a direct line, and a daily review of logged issues. Normal hours and email leave users stuck during the period of most confusion."},

// u9l1
{id:"u9l1-1",t:"u9l1",q:"You need to decide how a new reading workflow will support patient care in the health system. Whom do you consult FIRST?",a:["The CMIO, a physician who leads the clinical use of systems","The CIO, who leads technology for the whole organization and sets its standards","The department administrator, who manages the operating budget and staffing for the unit","The technologist supervisor, who checks image quality"],c:0,e:"The CMIO is the physician leader for how clinical systems are used. The CIO owns technology standards and enterprise budgets, and the administrator and technologist supervisor own other decisions."},
{id:"u9l1-2",t:"u9l1",q:"Who is responsible for finalizing the radiology report?",a:["The resident who drafts it, since the draft is the report","The attending, who is responsible for the patient and the report","The fellow, because of subspecialty training and a longer time in the program","The technologist who checked the image quality"],c:1,e:"Residents and fellows are physicians in training; the attending is the senior physician responsible for the patient and the final report. Workflow rules often route a study to a resident first and to the attending for finalization."},
{id:"u9l1-3",t:"u9l1",q:"You are asked to support a children's hospital. Which need shapes its workflow most?",a:["Review board approval for de-identified trial data and its naming rules","Heavy teaching file and resident education features for each case","Size-based protocols and dose sensitivity","Remote support for a single small clinic"],c:2,e:"Pediatrics means patients of very different sizes, so protocols depend on size and dose matters more. Teaching files, trial approval, and remote clinic support describe academic, research, and ambulatory settings."},
{id:"u9l1-4",t:"u9l1",q:"A group needs prior comparisons and measurements over time to follow tumors. Which specialty is this?",a:["Neurology, which reads brain and spine studies","Orthopedics, which compares bones and joints","Cardiology, which uses moving images and heart measurements","Oncology, which stages and follows cancer with measurements over time"],c:3,e:"Oncology depends on comparing priors and measuring change. Orthopedics, cardiology, and neurology each use imaging differently, and informatics designs their hanging protocols and tools accordingly."},
{id:"u9l1-5",t:"u9l1",q:"A hanging protocol dispute arises over neuroradiology layouts. Who usually owns the decision?",a:["The neuroradiology section chief, who leads that subspecialty","The department chair, who leads the whole radiology department and all its sections","The CIO, who sets technology standards for the entire organization and its sites","The PACS administrator, who runs the systems"],c:0,e:"A section chief leads a subspecialty inside a department, so layouts for that subspecialty are theirs to decide. The chair, CIO, and administrator support the decision but do not own it."},

// u9l2
{id:"u9l2-1",t:"u9l2",q:"Which plane divides the body into front and back parts?",a:["Axial, which divides the body into top and bottom parts","Coronal, which divides front from back","Sagittal, which divides the body into left and right parts","Lateral, which names a side view"],c:1,e:"Coronal divides front from back, axial divides top from bottom, and sagittal divides left from right. Lateral is a projection, not a plane."},
{id:"u9l2-2",t:"u9l2",q:"The order says left knee, but the study header says right. What is the BEST response?",a:["Route the study as normal, since laterality is a clerical field","Correct the header to match the order without checking the patient","Treat it as a patient safety error: hold the study and have the technologist verify against the order","Delete the study and ask for a new order"],c:2,e:"A wrong laterality can send care to the wrong side, so it is a safety error. Hold the study and have the technologist verify the facts before changing anything, instead of overwriting or ignoring the field."},
{id:"u9l2-3",t:"u9l2",q:"The beam passes through the patient from back to front. Which projection is this?",a:["Oblique","AP","Lateral","PA"],c:3,e:"PA is back to front and AP is front to back. A lateral beam passes from side to side, and an oblique beam is at an angle."},
{id:"u9l2-4",t:"u9l2",q:"A hanging protocol fails to place a CT series in the right window. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["The header's series or view value differs from the protocol's word","The workstation lacks support for the axial plane in its viewer software","The patient was scanned in a supine position on the table instead of prone","The study has too many images for the viewer to place in one window at once"],c:0,e:"Protocols match on header values such as series description, body part, and view position. A mismatch between the words in the header and the words in the protocol is a common reason a series is not placed."},
{id:"u9l2-5",t:"u9l2",q:"A radiologist asks for a particular sequence. Which modality uses that term?",a:["CT, where a sequence is a phase after contrast injection","MRI, where a sequence is a pulse and timing pattern","Mammography, where a sequence is a standard projection","Fluoroscopy, where it is a frame rate"],c:1,e:"In MRI a sequence is a pulse and timing pattern that sets the image contrast. Mammography uses views, and CT uses phases and series, so the same word can mean different things by modality."},

// u9l3
{id:"u9l3-1",t:"u9l3",q:"Which five questions must every downtime or change notice answer?",a:["Who, what, when, which system, and how many users","Who, what, where, which vendor, and how much it costs","Who, what, when, why, and how","Who, what, when, why, and which approver signed it"],c:2,e:"A notice answers who is affected, what is changing, when it starts and ends, why, and how users should act. If you cannot answer how, the notice is not ready."},
{id:"u9l3-2",t:"u9l3",q:"In a downtime notice, what should technologists see that radiologists need less?",a:["How referring clinicians can see reports","How to read studies in the backup viewer while the main system is unavailable","The sequence of upgrade steps and the back-out time for the work","Where to send images and how to log exams on paper while the system is down"],c:3,e:"Technologists scan during downtime, so they need to know where images go and how to record exams. Radiologists need reading access, IT needs the technical sequence, and clinicians need report access."},
{id:"u9l3-3",t:"u9l3",q:"A planned four hour PACS upgrade is coming. Which notification plan is BEST?",a:["A first notice weeks ahead, a reminder before, a day-of banner, and an all-clear when it ends","A single email on the day of the upgrade","A login banner shown only when the upgrade starts","An email to IT staff only"],c:0,e:"Early notice lets people plan, reminders reinforce it, several channels reach different roles, and an all-clear closes the event. One late message or an IT-only message leaves users surprised."},
{id:"u9l3-4",t:"u9l3",q:"An unplanned outage begins. What should the FIRST message contain?",a:["Nothing until the cause is known and confirmed by the vendor, so that the message is accurate and complete","A short message at once: what is down, what to use, who is working on it, and when the next update comes","A detailed root cause report for the chair","A note to the on-call analysts only"],c:1,e:"Early short messages reduce calls and confusion; naming the time of the next update tells people when to look. Waiting for a root cause leaves users guessing."},
{id:"u9l3-5",t:"u9l3",q:"After announcing a workflow change, how do you learn whether the message reached users?",a:["Count how many people opened the email and clicked the link to the full notice","Assume the message landed, since no one objected to it during the notice period","A short survey or floor walk after the change, plus the help-desk call trend","Ask the vendor whether its other customers noticed the same change in workflow"],c:2,e:"Closing the loop means asking users or watching what they do. Silence and email opens do not show that people understood what to do differently."},

// u9l4
{id:"u9l4-1",t:"u9l4",q:"The hospital's strategic goal is faster stroke treatment, and your department wants a PACS upgrade. What is the BEST way to present it?",a:["Wait for leaders to ask","Describe the technical features of the upgrade in detail","Request funding because the current system is old","State the goal in the organization's words, such as faster head CT reads, with a measure and its source"],c:3,e:"Alignment means tying the department's goal to the organization's plan and showing how it will be measured. Leaders fund what connects to their strategy, not what is described in technical terms."},
{id:"u9l4-2",t:"u9l4",q:"Which pair of feedback sources gives objective data and user opinion?",a:["Reporting tools for measured results and surveys for user opinion","Surveys for measured results and reporting tools for user opinion about the system","Two surveys with different questions, sent to different groups","Two reporting tools that measure the same thing"],c:0,e:"Reporting tools pull objective numbers from systems; surveys ask people what they think. Each shows something the other cannot, so a good review uses both."},
{id:"u9l4-3",t:"u9l4",q:"A survey returns an average rating from 6 of 60 radiologists. How should you report it?",a:["Report only the average rating, since it is the main result of the survey and the number leaders ask for","Report the rating together with the response rate so the reader can judge its weight","Discard the survey as invalid because too few radiologists answered it, and send a new one next year","Resend the survey until every radiologist answers"],c:1,e:"Report the response rate with the score; a result from a tenth of the group needs caution. Dropping it wastes information, and chasing a full response without changing the survey rarely helps."},
{id:"u9l4-4",t:"u9l4",q:"What is the BEST format for an update to hospital leadership?",a:["A slide for each system with its settings and version history","A full technical report with every KPI so that leaders can choose what to read","One page: goal, result, cost, risk, and one clear request","Verbal updates only, with no record"],c:2,e:"Leaders want the goal and result first, brief detail on cost and risk, and a clear request. Detail can follow if they ask."},
{id:"u9l4-5",t:"u9l4",q:"The dashboard shows turnaround within target, but a survey says radiologists find the system slow. What is the BEST action?",a:["Rerun the survey with fewer questions to see if the next result matches the dashboard","Trust the dashboard and set aside the survey, since numbers are more objective than opinion","Trust the survey and drop the dashboard, since users know best how the system performs","Investigate both, since the dashboard may miss a step such as viewer launch"],c:3,e:"Objective numbers and opinion can disagree because the measure misses something users feel. Investigate both, then adjust the measure or the system."},

// u10l1
{id:"u10l1-1",t:"u10l1",q:"Which event creates a training need?",a:["A system upgrade that changes screens and behavior users already know","A change in the format of the vendor's invoice to the department","A new backup schedule that runs overnight and changes nothing for users","A monthly report sent to the chair"],c:0,e:"Training is triggered by a new system, a workflow change, or an upgrade that changes what users do. Invisible changes and routine reports do not."},
{id:"u10l1-2",t:"u10l1",q:"200 users at 6 sites need to learn a short list of new tasks. Which delivery is BEST?",a:["Classroom training at the main site only, repeated for each shift and each role","E-learning modules and reference cards, with virtual sessions for questions","One hour in person at each of the six sites, delivered by the vendor's trainer","Vendor documentation sent by email with a request to read it before go-live"],c:1,e:"A large, dispersed audience with a short list of tasks fits self-paced modules and cards, with live virtual help. Classroom delivery to everyone is slow and costly, and documentation alone gives no practice."},
{id:"u10l1-3",t:"u10l1",q:"Which is a checkable instructional objective?",a:["The technologist is aware of the new workflow","The technologist understands how study lists work in the new system","The technologist can correct a patient name mismatch in the study list and confirm it in the audit trail","The technologist knows why names matter"],c:2,e:"A checkable objective states an action you can observe. Words like understands, aware, and knows cannot be tested, so a learner cannot show they have met them."},
{id:"u10l1-4",t:"u10l1",q:"What does a task analysis show in a training program?",a:["The hours each learner spent on the course","How satisfied learners were with the session and its materials","Which quiz questions were missed most often by the group","The steps of a job and where people hesitate or fail"],c:3,e:"Task analysis breaks a job into steps and records where people stop or err, which shows what to teach or redesign. Satisfaction is a survey measure, and quiz results and hours measure other things."},
{id:"u10l1-5",t:"u10l1",q:"One hour-long webinar is planned for radiologists, technologists, and clerks. What is the problem?",a:["Each role performs different tasks, so objectives and methods must be written by role","One hour is too short for any role to learn a new system well enough to use it every day","Webinars cannot teach software tasks, since learners cannot practice while they watch them","Clerks should be trained before the other roles"],c:0,e:"Training is built from the tasks each role performs. A single generic session wastes time for some roles and misses tasks for others."},

// u10l2
{id:"u10l2-1",t:"u10l2",q:"How should super-users be chosen and used?",a:["Pick the most junior person on each team, since they have time to spare for the work","Train a respected person in each area and shift to coach peers and report problems","Use one super-user for the whole site, who is on call by phone for every shift","Have the vendor trainer act as the only super-user"],c:1,e:"Super-users put trained help on every shift and in every area. Choose people colleagues trust, give them time, and a direct line to the project team. One person cannot cover all shifts."},
{id:"u10l2-2",t:"u10l2",q:"Staff need to follow ten steps in a fixed order at the workstation. Which format fits?",a:["A graphic poster with no steps on it","A one-hour lecture the week before go-live for all the staff","A written guide or a one-page reference card kept at the workstation","A recorded webinar of the vendor presentation, posted on the intranet"],c:2,e:"Ordered steps people look up are best in a written guide or a card where the work is done. A lecture is forgotten and a poster without steps does not help."},
{id:"u10l2-3",t:"u10l2",q:"Radiologists cannot leave the worklist for long. Which schedule is BEST?",a:["Sessions at the main site only","A single half-day class on a weekday morning for the whole department","Training sessions held months before go-live, while the project is still in planning","Short sessions on every shift, with a recorded version, held close to go-live"],c:3,e:"Training has to fit around clinical work: short sessions, offered on each shift, recorded for those who miss them, and close enough to go-live that people remember."},
{id:"u10l2-4",t:"u10l2",q:"Why provide a test system for training?",a:["So learners can practice tasks without touching live patient records","It reduces the license cost of the production system, which counts seats in use","It makes quizzes easier to grade","It replaces the need for super-users"],c:0,e:"A test system allows hands-on practice, including mistakes, without risk to patient data. It does not replace super-users or lower license costs."},
{id:"u10l2-5",t:"u10l2",q:"Eight technologists at one site must learn a task with many clicks. Which delivery is BEST?",a:["A recorded video only, which technologists can watch on any workstation they choose","In person, hands-on, in the test system","An e-learning module with no practice exercises at the end of the lesson","A written guide emailed to each technologist"],c:1,e:"A task with many clicks needs practice, and a small group at one site is easy to gather. Video, e-learning without practice, and a guide alone teach knowledge but not the hands-on skill."},

// u10l3
{id:"u10l3-1",t:"u10l3",q:"Training attendance sheets are full, yet calls about three screens double after go-live. What does this show?",a:["The help desk is understaffed and needs more people on each shift to handle the volume","Training succeeded, and the extra calls are normal growing pains of any new system","Attendance does not show skill, and the call trend points to task gaps to retrain","The screens should be removed"],c:2,e:"A sign-in sheet shows who attended, not who can do the job. Rising calls on particular tasks show where training missed, so retrain on those tasks."},
{id:"u10l3-2",t:"u10l3",q:"Which is an authentic assessment?",a:["A sign-in sheet for the session","A multiple-choice quiz on the policy","A survey of how confident learners feel","Correcting a mismatched patient name in the test system while an assessor uses a checklist"],c:3,e:"Authentic assessment has the learner perform a real task. A quiz tests knowledge, a survey opinion, and a sheet attendance."},
{id:"u10l3-3",t:"u10l3",q:"Calls about printing fall each week, but calls about correcting names stay flat. What is the BEST action?",a:["Add a refresher or card step on correcting names for those who need it","Retrain everyone on every task in the system, since training is a cycle","Conclude that training worked, since total calls fell from week to week","Remove the name correction screen"],c:0,e:"The flat topic marks a gap, so target it: a refresher, a card, or a video for the people who need it. Retraining everyone wastes time, and ignoring the trend leaves the problem."},
{id:"u10l3-4",t:"u10l3",q:"Why keep a record of who completed training?",a:["To rank employees against one another when annual performance reviews are held","To show who was trained before using the system, and who still needs it","To replace the assessment of skills, since a completed course proves competence","To reduce help-desk calls by listing the users who have not yet attended a session"],c:1,e:"A record shows an auditor or accreditor that staff were trained before use and lets you find those who missed it. It does not replace assessment."},
{id:"u10l3-5",t:"u10l3",q:"Technologists rate their confidence 4.8 of 5, but observation shows many fail at one step. What does this show?",a:["Training is fine and observation is unreliable for judging technologists","The survey is wrong and should be discarded in favor of the results of observation","Confidence and ability differ, so fix the step that observation exposed","Technologists should be retrained on every task"],c:2,e:"A survey gathers opinion; observation measures performance. When they disagree, believe the observed failure and fix that step."}
);
